sequenceDiagram
  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, ADR 0261, docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake, docs/reference/prepared-restore.md): freeze a development container, record the pages its processes had resident, move its memory to swap and leave its Route to wake it on the next visit. Defaults and nested-fleet Instances sleep under the same rule as clones (owner decision 2026-11-14). The hibernation sweep admits the same kind after an idle hour; the command admits it at once. Planned (ADR 0265, docs/reference/production-containers.md#sleep-and-wake): a production Instance's live release container sleeps by the same rule on its app-prod Node, and never takes the stop.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:sleep
  %% kind: instance.sleep
  %% ADR 0266: ordinary sleep and the idle sweep share one admitted Operation and one signed action, delivered only over the WebSocket.
  %% Executor sleep: record and fsync non-max subtree swap limits, open them, freeze through Incus local API, scan every process in parallel (readable mappings, pagemap bits 63 OR 62, excluding bit 58 guard markers), atomically fsync the plan, reclaim until memory.current settles or 2 s, apply the 32 MiB anonymous policy, write the asleep marker. Recovery keeps an existing valid plan. Discard removes plan and limits after stop/fresh boot; destroy deletes the Rust state folder without an order (signed destroy: task 5891).
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · sweep
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container
  Note over GW,N: Slice 4 proof: Rust sleep<br/>Incus freeze → scan → reclaim
  %%> Executor proof path:
  %%> - a durable executor.power_proof Operation signs sleep or discard; the observer only relays bytes
  %%> - Rust records non-max swap limits and fsyncs; Incus API freeze; parallel whole-subtree capture includes every readable present-or-swapped page (bits 63 and 62), excluding bit 58 guard markers inside readable mappings
  %%> - Rust publishes the marker before freeze, atomically fsyncs the plan, and gives reclaim 2 s to settle; the 32 MiB residual policy still applies
  %%> - discard drops the Rust plan and limits after stop or fresh boot; destroy removes the Rust state folder as plain cleanup; no production flow switches in this slice
  %%> - sleep and discard first wait up to 10 s for a stalled restore read of an earlier wake of the same target, else `instance.restore_readers_busy`
  CLI->>GW: instance:sleep INSTANCE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:sleep INSTANCE`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--json]`
  %%> Callers:
  %%> - the command, at once
  %%> - the idle sleep of the runtime hibernator, every 10 min, after it read no HTTP, wake or deployment activity for an hour and, for an Instance with a T3 server, no T3 activity and no running turn (docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake)
  %%> - planned: the same sweep and command for a production Instance; they act on its live release container, which has no T3 server
  opt the Instance has a T3 server
    Note over GW: Last read of T3 threads<br/>Conn thread index
    %%> When:
    %%> - after the refusals and the replay of a repeated request, before the Instance lock; within 3 seconds
    %%> - only while Conn and its index switch `ORBIT_CONN_THREAD_INDEX_POLL` are on; the sleep setting does not matter
    %%> - a default has no T3 server: nothing to read
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads the container T3 server's threads once more, so the index keeps them while the Instance sleeps (docs/reference/conn.md#thread-index)
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the index keeps its earlier rows; the sleep goes on
  end
  Note over GW: Admit the sleep
  %%> When:
  %%> - an active development container, a default and a nested-fleet Instance included; take the Instance lock
  %%> - planned: a production Instance's live release container; hot and old are never admitted, and the sweep reports `not_live` for them; an open deploy or rollback keeps the Instance awake (`busy`)
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - no container, a non-development Instance, an active task or lifecycle work starts no run
  %%> - planned: the reason `not_development` goes; a production Instance is admitted for its live only
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID
  alt signed power enabled at admission
    %% path: signed-sleep
    GW->>N: Publish signed sleep<br/>executor-order · WebSocket
    %%> - Check the loaded Caddy rule before any freeze and put up the marker before the app stops answering. Without a published Route there is no site to check.
    %%> - The executor owns freeze, capture, swap and the final marker. If sleep fails after the freeze, it reports the incomplete sleep and keeps the state for the same engine to wake. It never stops a nested fleet.
    %%> - The WebSocket is the only delivery; the Gateway publishes once, never relays the order over SSH, and starts no Swamp run.
    alt a receipt arrives
      Note over GW: Project sleep or failure<br/>one terminal Operation
      %%> - A known refusal or failure does not record asleep. Repeated deliveries return the first result without freezing twice.
      %%> - R1: a recorded `done` receipt is the sleep's outcome even when a later transport fails; the Operation succeeds and records asleep
      %%> - R3: the freezing engine owns the freeze; a cancelled or failed later sleep does not hand it to the other engine
      %%> - More than 32 MiB of anonymous RAM after reclaim takes the same stop rule as Python for a plain container. Report `sleep: stopped`, `prepared_restore: skipped`, reason `stopped` and the residual counters. The CLI says the next wake boots without restoring memory.
    else the publish fails
      %% path: publish-failed
      Note over GW: Fail, free the lock<br/>executor.publish_failed
      %%> - No Node received the order; the Operation fails at once.
    else no accepted line by 6 s
      %% path: not-accepted
      Note over GW: Fail, free the lock<br/>executor.not_accepted
      %%> - The order expired 5 s after signing, so it can act on no Node; nothing holds the fence (R2).
    else accepted, no result by 6 s
      %% path: result-unknown
      Note over GW: Interrupted, keep lock<br/>executor.result_unknown
      %%> - R2: the executor may have frozen the container, so the Instance lock stays and cancel is refused until a late receipt settles the Operation.
    end
  else signed power disabled at admission
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-sleep
  SW->>N: Sleep the container<br/>Ansible · tags prepared_sleep
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one phase: first installs the power helper from this commit when it differs and probes it, so a failed install changes nothing a visitor sees
  %%> - checks that Caddy's loaded site of the Instance has the asleep rule, else `instance.sleep_route_unready`; an Instance without a published Route has no site and nothing to check; the intent's `route` says which
  %%> - writes the asleep marker, so each request from now on waits while the Instance wakes
  %%> - runs `orbit-instance-power prepare` as the unit `orbit-instance-power-<id>`, the Instance's lock; a busy lock is retried 0.25 s apart, 40 times
  %%> - prepare reads the state from Incus first; running: removes the earlier plan and `sleep.json`, writes `max` to `memory.swap.max` of every cgroup of the subtree that holds 0 and records them in `limits.json` before the pause; `incus pause`
  %%> - prepare, frozen: lists `cgroup.procs` of the whole subtree, every process alike, and records each one's identity and the resident pages of its readable mappings; publishes the plan atomically; one `memory.reclaim`
  %%> - then runs `orbit-instance-power residual` until anonymous and file memory are 0, 20 calls 0.1 s apart, through the same call and busy rule as `prepare`; each call reads `memory.stat` once and stores it in `sleep.json`; the last one is what stays in RAM
  %%> - a frozen container stays as it is, keeps its plan for the next wake and reports the earlier sleep's result from `sleep.json`; a stopped one stays stopped, its old plan is discarded, `not_applicable`
  %%> - a busy lock is only systemd's `UnitExists` refusal; a helper crash fails the phase with `instance.power_helper_failed`, never retried
  %%> - a nested-fleet Instance gets no plan: `skipped`, `placement_unproven`
  %%> Why:
  %%> - one phase: each Ansible start costs about 4 s
  %%> - the marker comes before the freeze: a request during the freeze goes to the activation check, not to a frozen app
  %%> - the plan is taken frozen, so it is exact; the reclaim follows it, so nothing stays in RAM by design
  %%> - with only the outer cgroup open, 1,531 MiB of a nested default stayed in RAM: a cgroup with 0 keeps its pages
  %%> - a cgroup swaps only as far as its ancestors allow, and Incus writes 0 unless `limits.memory.swap` is a byte size: a nested-fleet container has it equal to its memory, so its own cgroup never caps the swap of its fleet's Instances
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - no HTTP request, no command in the container, no app file read, no route or process-name choice
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - a capture that is unsupported, too large (docs/reference/prepared-restore.md#bounds) or too slow leaves no plan; the sleep goes on, `skipped` with its reason
  %%> - a failed swap write or pause closes each limit it opened and removes the marker, `instance.container_freeze_failed`; the container serves as before
  opt more than 32 MiB anonymous stays in RAM, same phase
    alt a nested-fleet Instance
      Note over N: Resume, never stop<br/>drop plan, restore, close swap
      %%> Why:
      %%> - a stop loses the Club key in /run and the tunnel `wg-nested`; only a deployment or a start gives them back
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the container runs again, swap closes on each cgroup the freeze opened, the marker goes; the run fails `instance.container_freeze_failed`
      %%> - the Instance serves as before; free swap and run the command again
    else a production live, planned
      Note over N: Resume, never stop<br/>drop plan, restore, close swap
      %%> Why:
      %%> - a stopped live would cold-boot its Processes and timers at the next visit (ADR 0265)
      %%> Result:
      %%> - live runs again, swap closes on each cgroup the freeze opened, the marker goes; the run fails `instance.container_freeze_failed`
      %%> - the sweep keeps live awake for one idle window with `swap_full`
    else any other Instance
      Note over N: Stop the container<br/>drop plan, restore, incus stop
      %%> Why:
      %%> - swap is full; a frozen container would hold RAM, which a sleep exists to free
      %%> Result:
      %%> - `sleep: stopped`; `discard --plan-only` dropped the plan before the resume, so no prefetch ran while swap is full; `skipped`, `stopped`; the next wake boots it behind the same marker
      %%> On failure:
      %%> - the resumed container runs: swap closes again, the marker goes, `instance.container_freeze_failed`
    end
  end
  alt the run passes
    %% path: main
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
    Note over GW: Record asleep
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `stopped` for a frozen and a stopped container; the result says `sleep: frozen` or `sleep: stopped`, and `swap_subtree`, the number of inner cgroups the freeze opened
    %%> - `prepared_restore`: `ready` with the plan's processes, bytes and `prepare_seconds`, or `unsupported`, `skipped` or `not_applicable` with a reason; `residual_anon_bytes` and `residual_file_bytes` from the last read
    %%> - `t3_turn` is `idle` when the idle sleep read T3 first, `no_server` for an Instance without a T3 server, whoever started the sleep, and `not_checked` for the command
    %%> - the Conn layer shows its T3 server asleep; the hourly renewal skips it
    %%> - planned, a production live: Caddy holds each request that meets the marker while the wake runs (wake.mmd); live's swap opened for the sleep, and a running live never swaps
  else a step fails
    %% path: failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - `power_state` stays as it was. A marker left on a running container is harmless: the next request wakes the Instance, which removes it
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - the same command again
  end
  end
  loop until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: Status and result
  end
